bution Label</th><th>Attribution Score</th><th>Word Importance</th><tr><td><text style=\"padding-right:2em\"><b>N/A</b></text></td><td><text style=\"padding-right:2em\"><b>Generated Text (1.00)</b></text></td><td><text style=\"padding-right:2em\"><b>Attribution</b></text></td><td><text style=\"padding-right:2em\"><b>5942016.50</b></text></td><td><mark style=\"background-color: hsl(0, 75%, 60%); opacity:1.0;                     line-height:1.75\"><font color=\"black\"> Paris                    </font></mark><mark style=\"background-color: hsl(0, 75%, 60%); opacity:1.0;                     line-height:1.75\"><font color=\"black\"> Ġis                    </font></mark><mark style=\"background-color: hsl(120, 75%, 50%); opacity:1.0;                     line-height:1.75\"><font color=\"black\"> Ġthe                    </font></mark><mark style=\"background-color: hsl(120, 75%, 50%); opacity:1.0;                     line-height:1.75\"><font color=\"black\"> Ġcapital                    </font></mark><mark style=\"background-color: hsl(120, 75%, 50%); opacity:1.0;                     line-height:1.75\"><font color=\"black\"> Ġof                    </font></mark><mark style=\"background-color: hsl(120, 75%, 50%); opacity:1.0;                     line-height:1.75\"><font color=\"black\"> ĠFrance                    </font></mark></td><tr></table>"]},"metadata":{},"execution_count":6}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["This block of code focuses on creating a clear, high-contrast heatmap of the token attribution values computed earlier, while ensuring that the visual output is clean and professional. It begins by setting consistent white backgrounds for all plots using Matplotlib and Seaborn styling options, which makes the visualisation easier to read and more suitable for exporting into reports or presentations.\n","\n","The attribution tensor is then prepared by converting it into a NumPy array and replacing any NaN values with zeros to avoid display errors. To make the colour contrast meaningful, the code scales the colour range symmetrically around zero, using the 95th percentile of attribution magnitudes to prevent outliers from dominating the colour scale.\n","\n","Finally, a heatmap is drawn where the x-axis corresponds to the tokens in the input sentence and the y-axis corresponds to the embedding dimensions. A diverging “coolwarm” colour map highlights positive versus negative contributions, with a colour bar indicating attribution strength."],"metadata":{"id":"-XxHQmMHurpl"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["import numpy as np\n","import matplotlib as mpl\n","import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n","import seaborn as sns\n","\n","sns.reset_defaults()\n","plt.style.use('default')\n","sns.set_style(\"white\")\n","mpl.rcParams['figure.facecolor'] = 'white'\n","mpl.rcParams['axes.facecolor']   = 'white'\n","mpl.rcParams['savefig.facecolor']= 'white'\n","\n","# Prepare data (avoid NaNs)\n","attr = attributions.squeeze().detach().cpu().numpy()\n","attr = np.nan_to_num(attr, nan=0.0)\n","\n","# Improve color contrast: symmetric limits around 0\n","vmax = np.percentile(np.abs(attr), 95)  # robust scaling\n","fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 6))\n","sns.heatmap(\n","    attr.T,\n","    cmap='coolwarm',\n","    center=0,\n","    vmin=-vmax, vmax=vmax,           # boosts colors away from grey center\n","    xticklabels=sentence.split(),\n","    cbar_kws={'label': 'Attribution'},\n","    ax=ax\n",")\n","ax.set_facecolor('white')            # in-axes background\n","\n","plt.xlabel('Tokens')\n","plt.ylabel('Embedding Dimension')\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()\n","\n","# If exporting, use:\n","# plt.savefig(\"ig_heatmap.png\", dpi=300, bbox_inches=\"tight\", facecolor='white')\n"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":495},"id":"8iodAcOQx-c1","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1757427888426,"user_tz":-420,"elapsed":1049,"user":{"displayName":"Harriet","userId":"08169952984174915407"}},"outputId":"67d25f3b-9c5b-4e1f-ee65-af3cdedc2f37"},"execution_count":7,"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 1200x600 with 2 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["This section demonstrates attention rollout, a method for visualising how the model distributes attention between tokens in the input sentence. From the model outputs, the final layer’s attention weights are extracted and detached from the computation graph, then converted into a NumPy array for easier manipulation. Because there are multiple attention heads, the code averages their values to produce a single attention matrix that summarises how strongly each token attends to every other token.\n","\n","A heatmap is then generated using Seaborn, with both the x-axis and y-axis labelled by the tokens in the sentence. The resulting plot shows pairwise attention scores: warmer colours indicate stronger attention from one token to another, while cooler colours indicate weaker attention. This provides an interpretable map of the model’s focus, revealing which words it considers most relevant to others when forming its internal representations.\n","\n","In short, this step allows us to see how the GPT-2 model connects words to one another through its attention mechanism, offering insight into the structure of its reasoning over the input."],"metadata":{"id":"o7DJF2NTus61"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Attention Rollout\n","attentions = outputs.attentions[-1].detach().squeeze().numpy()\n","attention_mean = attentions.mean(axis=0)\n","\n","plt.figure(figsize=(8, 6))\n","sns.heatmap(attention_mean, xticklabels=sentence.split(), yticklabels=sentence.split(), cmap='coolwarm')\n","plt.title('Attention Rollout')\n","plt.xlabel('Tokens')\n","plt.ylabel('Tokens')\n","plt.show()"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":564},"id":"Q7N-1bc_xtoS","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1757427888707,"user_tz":-420,"elapsed":270,"user":{"displayName":"Harriet","userId":"08169952984174915407"}},"outputId":"a7f3804b-6e04-4b98-ccfa-21beb005964b"},"execution_count":8,"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 800x600 with 2 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Attention Rollout\n","attentions = outputs.attentions[-1].detach().squeeze().numpy()\n","attention_mean = attentions.mean(axis=0)\n","\n","plt.figure(figsize=(8, 6))\n","vmin = np.percentile(attention_mean, 5)   # 5th percentile\n","vmax = np.percentile(attention_mean, 95)  # 95th percentile\n","\n","sns.heatmap(\n","    attention_mean,\n","    xticklabels=sentence.split(),\n","    yticklabels=sentence.split(),\n","    cmap='binary',            # white = low, black = high\n","    cbar=True,\n","    vmin=attention_mean.min(),\n","    vmax=attention_mean.max()\n",")\n","\n","\n","\n","plt.title('Attention Rollout')\n","plt.xlabel('Tokens')\n","plt.ylabel('Tokens')\n","plt.show()\n"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":564},"id":"vtO8Qvya3kUn","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1757428175371,"user_tz":-420,"elapsed":1217,"user":{"displayName":"Harriet","userId":"08169952984174915407"}},"outputId":"74676e3e-d995-422e-dfc4-cedb2e6f81f9"},"execution_count":18,"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 800x600 with 2 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["This block of code provides a simple demonstration of activation patching, a technique often used in mechanistic interpretability to test how specific tokens contribute to a model’s behaviour. The idea is to intervene in the model’s internal activations and observe what changes downstream.\n","\n","Here, the hidden representation for the first token in the sentence (corresponding to “Paris”) is replaced with a zero vector, effectively erasing its contribution. The modified activations are then visualised with a heatmap, showing how the embedding dimensions look after this intervention.\n","\n","While this example is simplified, the principle illustrates how researchers can “patch” activations — by replacing, modifying, or swapping them — to study the causal role of particular tokens, layers, or features. This technique helps identify which parts of a model’s internal representations are responsible for specific outputs, and is a cornerstone of modern interpretability research."],"metadata":{"id":"8T5cgKE0uzmt"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Activation Patching (Example Conceptual Demo)\n","# Replace activations of \"Paris\" token with zero vector\n","patched_activations = activations.copy()\n","patched_activations[0] = 0\n","\n","plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n","sns.heatmap(patched_activations, cmap=\"viridis\")\n","plt.title('Activation Patching: \"Paris\" Token Patched')\n","plt.xlabel('Embedding Dimension')\n","plt.ylabel('Token Position')\n","plt.show()"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":577},"id":"ukMz6H0Zx0ha","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1757427889615,"user_tz":-420,"elapsed":907,"user":{"displayName":"Harriet","userId":"08169952984174915407"}},"outputId":"0b1af8f5-7cc2-48cb-b7eb-504e29c3802c"},"execution_count":9,"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 1000x600 with 2 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["This section gives a conceptual illustration of circuit analysis, which in mechanistic interpretability refers to studying how information flows and interacts within a model. Instead of looking only at individual token activations, here we compute an interaction matrix by taking the dot product of the activation vectors for all tokens in the sentence. This creates a measure of how strongly each token’s representation aligns with or interacts with every other token’s representation.\n","\n","The resulting heatmap plots tokens along both axes, with colour intensity indicating the degree of interaction between pairs of words. For example, in the sentence “Paris is the capital of France,” we might expect stronger interactions between “Paris” and “France” than between unrelated words, since the model likely encodes their semantic relationship more closely.\n","\n","Although simplified, this demonstration captures the spirit of circuit analysis: identifying structured pathways or “circuits” inside neural networks that explain how specific relationships or reasoning patterns emerge. By mapping these token-to-token interactions, researchers can begin to uncover the internal mechanisms that drive model behaviour, moving from opaque activations toward interpretable structures."],"metadata":{"id":"3xJA1I9mu9Sg"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":10,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":564},"id":"S9jGfdxEfO_i","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1757427890735,"user_tz":-420,"elapsed":1117,"user":{"displayName":"Harriet","userId":"08169952984174915407"}},"outputId":"5716b92a-6898-4510-8815-5c4fcb084b48"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 800x600 with 2 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"source":["# Circuit Analysis - Visualising how tokens might interact\n","interaction_matrix = np.dot(activations, activations.T)\n","\n","plt.figure(figsize=(8, 6))\n","sns.heatmap(interaction_matrix, xticklabels=sentence.split(), yticklabels=sentence.split(), cmap='magma')\n","plt.title('Circuit Analysis: Token Interaction')\n","plt.xlabel('Tokens')\n","plt.ylabel('Tokens')\n","plt.show()\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","source":[],"metadata":{"id":"_9MiM8POx5ps","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1757427890738,"user_tz":-420,"elapsed":2,"user":{"displayName":"Harriet","userId":"08169952984174915407"}}},"execution_count":10,"outputs":[]}]}